### 5. Gap Analysis

> **GAP [CRITICAL]:** The 70-80% value figure ("leaving 70-80% of the value on the table") is asserted without a sourced study, named research, or methodology. Marcus attributes it to "research across hundreds of AI deployments" — but no firm, publication, or dataset is named. Without this, the article's central claim is unsupported. The draft either needs the citation, a softer qualifier ("in our experience"), or explicit acknowledgment that this is a practitioner estimate. If left unresolved, the article's lead argument is vulnerable to easy dismissal.

> **GAP [CRITICAL]:** The 2.5-4x ROI over three years claim is similarly unanchored. This figure will attract CFO attention, which means it will also attract CFO scrutiny. If the article cannot point to a source, it either needs a hedge ("in the engagements we track") or a client composite that supports the range. Unresolved, this number weakens credibility at exactly the moment it should establish it.

> **GAP [CRITICAL]:** No specific client stories exist in this transcript. Every example is a composite pattern. The article as outlined will read as a framework document — authoritative but abstract. A single anonymized client arc (problem, decision, measurable outcome) would anchor all three of Marcus's major claims in reality. Without it, a skeptical reader has no reason to trust that the framework works in practice.

> **GAP [CRITICAL]:** The $1 change management per $2 technology spend ratio is stated as a prescription but has no evidentiary basis offered. Where does that ratio come from? Is it Marcus's rule of thumb? Derived from client engagements? Industry research? The article will be shareable precisely because of this specificity — and its credibility depends on being able to defend it.

> **GAP [NICE-TO-HAVE]:** "Organizational intelligence" is named as the third dimension of AI value but never defined concretely. The article will introduce a three-part framework and leave one-third of it opaque. At minimum, one concrete example of what organizational intelligence looks like in practice would close this.

> **GAP [NICE-TO-HAVE]:** The AI value scorecard is mentioned — leading vs. lagging indicators, with examples — but never shown in full. A complete scorecard example (even illustrative) would be a high-value visual callout and would make the article genuinely actionable rather than advisory.

> **GAP [NICE-TO-HAVE]:** Marcus says organizations with a "structured AI value framework" are "three times more likely to successfully scale beyond pilot." Same issue as the other statistics — source not named. Less central to the argument, but still worth flagging.

